<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.NFC" />

    <!--
      THE BACKGROUND PUSH DOES NOT EXIST WITHOUT THIS LINE (TASK-225), AND ITS ABSENCE IS
      SILENT. A NORMAL, install-time permission: no dialog, nothing to deny, nothing on the
      clock-in path.

      `JobScheduler.schedule()` on a JobInfo carrying `setRequiredNetworkType(...)` throws

          SecurityException: android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE required for jobs
                             with a connectivity constraint

      This app declared INTERNET and not this, so EVERY schedule() threw, sync/SyncScheduler
      caught it in a `runCatching {}` that discarded the result, and the offline push shipped
      DEAD: `adb shell cmd jobscheduler get-job-state io.github.qwadratic.NFCTimeSheets 225`
      answered "unknown" after every offline tap. Nothing in the app, on the screen, or in
      any source-reading check could have said so — which is TASK-225's own bug rebuilt one
      layer down, and it was found only by asking a real device
      (`demo/prove-offline-push.mjs` § 1).

      Two guards now keep it: SyncScheduler.Armed.Refused carries the platform's answer to
      the screen instead of dropping it, and `android/checks/manifest-check.sh` fails the
      build when a connectivity constraint exists without this permission.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Self-update (this iteration). A normal, install-time permission below Android 8;
      on 8+ it also requires the per-app "install unknown apps" toggle the worker (or
      whoever set the phone up) must grant once in Settings — update/UpdateReadiness.kt
      checks that BEFORE ever handing the downloaded APK to the installer, and sends the
      worker straight to that one setting rather than letting the install silently do
      nothing. THE TRUST BOUNDARY IS THE SIGNING KEY, NOT THIS PERMISSION: this only lets
      the app OFFER an install; Android's own PackageInstaller still refuses any APK not
      signed with this app's own upload key (keystore.properties, gitignored) — that
      refusal is the desired behaviour, not a bug to route around.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!--
      The ongoing "you are clocked in" notification and the reminder ladder.

      Runtime-requested on API 33+ ONLY, AFTER the first successful clock-in, from the
      shift screen (core/ShiftSignal.shouldAskForNotifications, which core-check pins).
      NEVER at launch and NEVER on the tap path: a worker asked at a door at 06:02 with
      gloves on says no once, permanently. A refusal is a weaker signal and one sentence,
      never a blocked tap.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      A reboot clears every notification. This is a NORMAL, install-time permission: no
      dialog, nothing to deny, nothing on the clock-in path. BootReceiver reads the open
      shift out of SQLite and reposts. See notify/Receivers.kt.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!--
      THERE IS NO FOREGROUND SERVICE HERE AND THERE MUST NOT BE ONE.

      An FGS keeps a PROCESS alive; nothing in this app needs one. setUsesChronometer(true)
      makes the SYSTEM tick the elapsed time, and the shift's truth is on the server. The
      notification is owned by NotificationManager, so it already survives a task-swipe and
      process death - strictly better than an FGS, which OEM battery managers kill.

      And it would buy ZERO extra visibility: Google's own docs put non-exempt FGS
      notifications inside the same POST_NOTIFICATIONS gate. In exchange it costs a
      `specialUse` subtype property, a Play App-content declaration with a demonstration
      video, and review - on a personal Play account (decision-27).

      Adding any FOREGROUND_SERVICE* permission is a review-gate BLOCK. The reasoning is
      in notify/ShiftSignals.kt; core-check.kt asserts this manifest stays clean.

      Not requested either: SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM / USE_EXACT_ALARM. The reminder ladder
      uses AlarmManager.setWindow, because nobody cares whether the 5-hour nudge lands at
      11:02 or 11:14, and Play polices exact alarms.
    -->

    <!--
      required="true": this app is a tag reader and nothing else. A phone without NFC
      cannot clock anyone in, so Play should not offer it. NfcReadiness.UNSUPPORTED still
      exists because a sideloaded install bypasses this filter.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.nfc" android:required="true" />

    <application
        android:name=".TimeSheetsApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.TimeSheets">

        <!--
          ONE tap surface. Both dispatch paths converge here, so TapInbox's 3-second
          window can collapse a physical tap that gets delivered twice.

          singleTask + onNewIntent: a tap while the app is already open must reuse the
          running instance, or the worker ends up with two stacked copies and a queue
          split across them.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              THE TAG PATH ON ANDROID 16+.

              "Starting Android 16, scanning NFC tags that store URL links will trigger the
              ACTION_VIEW intent... Use Android app links to open your app for the URL."

              So the mechanism is exactly the iOS universal-link model, against the exact
              URI already written on the walls: https://<host>/t?l=<uuid>. ZERO tag rewrites.

              ${tagHost} is injected from branding.properties. Gradle FAILS the build on an
              unresolved placeholder — deliberately unlike Xcode, where an undefined build
              setting expands to the empty string and silently kills the association.

              autoVerify="true" makes Android fetch https://<host>/.well-known/assetlinks.json.
              That file is ALREADY SERVED, and its sha256_cert_fingerprints array is still
              empty, so verification WILL FAIL and every tap WILL open Chrome until the
              owner pastes both Play fingerprints (upload + Play App Signing) into
              ops/branding.json. That is step 21 of the plan and it is not an agent's to do.
              Proof, on the device: adb shell pm get-app-links <applicationId>

              android:path="/t" and not pathPrefix: /t is the whole path. A prefix would
              also claim /tags, /t-anything and any future route under it.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${tagHost}" android:path="/t" />
                <!-- The server serves both /t and /t/ rather than redirecting: a redirect
                     breaks the App-Link handoff. Claim both. -->
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${tagHost}" android:path="/t/" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          THE TAG PATH ON ANDROID <= 15, where an NDEF URI record fires
          ACTION_NDEF_DISCOVERED instead of ACTION_VIEW.

          A SEPARATE activity on purpose, and this is not tidiness:

          TRAP — Android 17 (API 37) requires an NFC-dispatched activity to be protected by
          android.permission.DISPATCH_NFC_MESSAGE when targetSdk > 36. `android:permission`
          restricts who may START an activity, so putting it on MainActivity would stop the
          launcher and the browser from opening the app at all. It can only ever go on an
          activity that NOTHING BUT the NFC service starts — which is this one.

          WHEN targetSdk MOVES TO 37, add exactly this attribute to THIS activity, and to
          no other:
              android:permission="android.permission.DISPATCH_NFC_MESSAGE"
          Adding it TODAY breaks Android <= 16, where that permission does not exist and
          therefore nobody holds it — i.e. it would break precisely the devices this
          activity exists for.

          Deliberately NOT filtering ACTION_TAG_DISCOVERED: deprecated in Android 17, and
          "your application has a low probability of starting" — it is the catch-all that
          makes the app fight every other NFC app on the phone.

          noHistory + excludeFromRecents: this is a doorway, not a screen.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".NfcTapActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:noHistory="true"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.NoDisplay">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.nfc.action.NDEF_DISCOVERED" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${tagHost}" android:path="/t" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${tagHost}" android:path="/t/" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          A rung of the reminder ladder fired. exported="false": only AlarmManager, acting
          on a PendingIntent we created, ever reaches it. Nothing outside the app may post
          a notification claiming a worker is still clocked in.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notify.ShiftReminderReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          Reboot recovery. exported must be TRUE for a system broadcast to be delivered,
          and the receiver checks the action itself before touching anything.

          BOOT_COMPLETED only, and NOT LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED: that one is delivered solely
          to directBootAware components, and a direct-boot component cannot read
          timesheets.db, which lives in credential-encrypted storage. Declaring it would be
          a filter that never fires, describing a recovery that never happens. So the
          notification comes back at first unlock, not at power-on.

          NOT delivered to a force-stopped app. Named here because it is the one recovery
          gap that cannot be closed from inside the app.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notify.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          THE OFFLINE PUSH (TASK-225). A JobService, which is NOT a foreground service and
          needs no FOREGROUND_SERVICE permission: the platform binds it, runs it for a few
          seconds when there is a network, and unbinds. It holds no process open.

          android:permission="android.permission.BIND_JOB_SERVICE" is REQUIRED by the
          platform — JobScheduler refuses to schedule against a service without it — and it
          is also the security property: only the system holds that permission, so nothing
          on the phone can start a push on our behalf.

          setPersisted(true) in sync/SyncScheduler.kt is why this survives a reboot, and it
          rides on the RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED permission already declared above for the
          notification. No new permission was added for this feature.

          exported="false": the system starts it through the job it was handed, and nothing
          else may.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".sync.ShiftSyncJob"
            android:exported="false"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_JOB_SERVICE" />

        <!--
          Explicit scan, the fallback for phones where the OS never dispatches the tag.
          exported="false": nothing outside this app has any business starting it, and it
          is reached only from a button inside the signed-in log screen.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".nfc.ScanActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="@string/scan_title" />

        <!--
          WRITE A TAG. exported="false", no intent filter, no NFC filter of any kind: this
          activity is reached only from a button inside the app, and it takes tags through
          reader mode while it is in the foreground.

          IT MUST NEVER GET AN NDEF_DISCOVERED FILTER. A tag lying on a desk would then be
          able to launch the screen that OVERWRITES tags, and a tap intended as a clock-in
          would land on a writer. Reader mode is foreground-only and torn down in onPause,
          which is exactly the property wanted here.

          singleTop: presenting a second tag must reuse the running screen rather than stack
          a second writer holding a second minted id.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".nfc.WriteTagActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:label="@string/write_title" />

        <!--
          THE TEST SCAN (operator), decision-47. exported="false", no intent filter, no NFC
          filter of any kind — same reasoning as WriteTagActivity above: reached only from a
          button inside the app, reader mode only while it is foregrounded and only once an
          operator has picked which zone is being tested.

          IT MUST NEVER GET AN NDEF_DISCOVERED FILTER, for the same reason WriteTagActivity
          must not: a tag lying on a desk would be able to launch this screen on its own.

          singleTop: presenting a second card must reuse the running screen, not stack a
          second verify session pointed at a stale zone selection.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".nfc.VerifyZoneActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:label="@string/verify_title" />

        <!--
          THE UPDATE SCREEN FOR AN OPERATOR-ONLY PHONE (TASK-254). exported="false" and no
          intent filter of ANY kind, same clause as the two screens above: it is reached
          only from a button inside the app.

          singleTop: two presses must not stack two update screens over one DownloadManager
          row.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.UpdateActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:label="@string/update_title" />

    </application>
</manifest>
